-- Source module: Solutions.KIL6Den
/-!
# KIL6: Lemma 12 as a uniform IL program — the list denotations

The IL program of KIL6 realises the Lemma 12 interchange with the following data flow (array
model, `AModelL12`):

1. **binary padding** `binShape … (2^u) → binShape … (q^e)`: two one-field paddings
   (`padL`, `embed_binShape`);
2. **high swaps**: `r = min ρ e` single-digit interchanges (`highSwaps`);
3. if `n = e - r > 0`, the **middle part** `l12mid`: the row field is the prefix
   `Pre ++ [q]^r` (volume `vol Pre · q^r`), padded to `padRows W Kp (q^r)` rows (`padL`); the
   Prop. 11 oracle runs on the pieces (`runPieces … pieceLevels`), with the gap
   `G ++ [q]^r` (the already interchanged high digits of the second chunk belong to the gap);
   the padding is scanned away (`unpadL`);
4. **binary extraction** (two `unpadL`).

Compared with `AModelL12.l12q` this variant needs no joining rotation: the interchanged high
digits `d⁺` simply stay inside the gap.  `l12v_correct` proves that it is the interchange
`swapPos` of the two `[2^u]` fields, from the same oracle specification `OracleSpec`.

The list functions `padL` / `unpadL` are the one-field embeddings / extractions in the chunk
form the IL loops produce (`embed_eq_padL`, `extract_eq_unpadL`).
-/

